── `catch` AROUND A FAILURE THAT BELONGS TO THE CALLER ── E0506_catch_around_a_failure_that_belongs_to_the_caller.lt:8:9  [E0506]

`fallback`'s failures belong to the caller, but this `catch` catches them:

  8 │     n + fallback()
    │         ^^^^^^^^^^

The failures of a function parameter belong to your caller, unless its type
names their error. Write `raise<E>` in the parameter's row to handle them here.

To fix it, handle `fallback`'s failures of type `ParseError`: write
`raise<ParseError>` in its row:

  5 │ fn safe(text: str, fallback: fn() -> i64 / raise<ParseError>) -> i64 {
    │                                         ++++++++++++++++++++
